+++ b/ops/known-issues.md
@@ -159,6 +159,26 @@ requirement itself is unchanged. Takes effect on the conductor's next restart.
 matches the YAML, both the original defect and this regression are closed. If it is skipped again,
 my instruction is not the cause and the planner's file-writing step needs looking at directly.
 
+
+**PRECONDITION ON THAT TEST — it is invalid unless the conductor has been RESTARTED first
+(noted 2026-09-02 21:30Z).** The corrected instruction is committed but NOT loaded: the conductor
+runs as a long-lived `--loop` process (pid 1301127, started 15:06Z) and reads
+`scripts/research_conductor.py` only at startup. The both-files-required fix landed at 21:15Z,
+after that.
+
+So if .605 plans on the current process and skips the document again, that is NOT evidence my
+instruction was innocent — it is evidence the old instruction is still in memory. Drawing the
+stated conclusion from an unrestarted run would clear my change on the strength of a test that
+never ran it. The test above requires: restart the conductor, THEN observe the next planning run.
+
+A restart was deliberately deferred at 21:30Z because a codex child was 41 minutes into a task and
+a restart kills the in-flight child. The next planning run is milestone .605, which cannot begin
+until .604's seven tasks finish, so there is time. The restart must happen in a gap between tasks
+and BEFORE .605 plans.
+
+Recorded because the failure mode is silent and self-flattering: forget the restart, watch .605
+skip the document, and conclude the regression was never mine.
+
